    {
      "sectionId": "section-firbolg-versions",
      "heading": "Firbolg Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "firbolg-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-firbolg-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Firbolg effectively has three versions. The original version of the Firbolg was published in Volo’s Guide to Monsters, and was reprinted without changes in Explorer’s Guide to Wildemount."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-firbolg-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules in Volo’s Guide to Monsters gave use the second version of the Firbolg. This was a big benefit to the Firbolg because their Str/Wis increases were extremely limiting in terms of build options."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-firbolg-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Monsters of the Multiverse gave us an updated version of the Firbolg. Their innate spellcasting no longer recharges on a short rest, but Hidden Step now works PB/long rest rather than once per short rest. This allows you to use it repeatedly in short succession when needed."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf",
      "heading": "Speech of Beast and Leaf",
      "sourceAnchor": "speech-of-beast-and-leaf",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Firbolg’s Speech of Beast and Leaf feature is worded very simply, but the\n\texact applications might be confusing. Speech of Beast and Leaf does not\n\treplicate the effects of the Speak With Animals and Speak With Plants spells,\n\tand it works differently from the Gnome’s Speak With Small Beasts feature.\n\tSpeech of Beast and Leaf is more akin to Kronk talking to squirrels, but you\n\tcan’t understand the animal or plant in return."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The text of the ability also doesn’t specifically state that Speech of Beast\n\tand Leaf only applies to creatures, so it’s not explicitly stated how it\n\tapplies to inanimate plants like a mundane tree or something. But with some\n\tcritical thinking we can work it out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Speaking to beasts is the easy part. “Beasts” is a creature type which\n\tincludes things like animals and giant bugs, ranging from the smallest rat to\n\tthe largest dinosaur. Since creatures like mundane bugs (house flies, normal\n\tspiders, etc.) aren’t given stat blocks it’s not totally clear if you can talk\n\tto them, but as a DM I would allow it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Speaking to plants is more complicated. Without magic, most mundane plants are inanimate and don’t have senses like humanoids do. Your DM might allow mundane plants to respond to extremely simple commands, like asking a venus fly trap to open or shut, but anything beyond the plant’s normal capabilities would require magic no matter how charming you were. But hey, you can tell plants that they’re pretty and they’ll understand you, and that has to count for something."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Plant creatures are another story, having more in common with beasts than\n\twith mundane plants (mechanically, at least). You might encounter a shambling\n\tmound and convince it not to eat you rather than getting into a fight with\n\twhat amounts to a compost elemental. Most plant creatures don’t speak a\n\tlanguage, so this is a wholly unique option for handling them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-of-beast-and-leaf-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "To make best use of Speech of Beast and Leaf, you’ll need to invest in Face\n\tskills like Persuasion. Without a natural Charisma increase, that’s an unusual\n\trole for the Firbolg. But spending one skill proficiency on Persuasion may be\n\tenough to mostly negate plant creatures as threats to your party, which might\n\tbe worth the investment in some games. If you’re using the custom origin\n\trules, you can easily build your Firbolg as a Face and address threats from\n\tplants and animals with ease."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Firbolg Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the exception of Speech of Beast and Leaf, the Artificer can already do\n\teverything that the Firbolg offers, and artificers typically dump Charisma, so\n\tspeaking to plants and animals is likely not helpful. The innate spellcasting\n\tdoes add additional spells on a class that doesn’t get full spellcasting, but\n\tthat doesn’t make this a great option."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There is no overlap between the Firbolg’s traits and the Barbarian’s capabilities. Their innate spellcasting is mostly useless for the Barbarian (Hidden Step works, but it’s not especially helpful), and Speech of Beast and leaf is nearly unusable on a class that dumps Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Firbolg’s innate spellcasting complements the Bard’s spellcasting nicely, and the ability to cast Disguise Self means that you don’t need to spend a spell known to learn it. The Bard’s high Charisma and access to Expertise work very well with Speech of Beast and Leaf, allowing you to easily solve many problems by talking to stuff."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Firbolg’s innate spellcasting is Wisdom-based, so a Wisdom-based spellcaster is a natural fit. But the more interesting part of the combination is access to Detect Magic, Disguise Self, and invisibility (the effect, not the spell). Only trickery clerics get access to Disguise Self and Invisibility, so the extra spells allow other clerics to be a little bit sneaky."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Perhaps the most obvious class choice, though not an especially powerful one. The Firbolg’s access to Disguise Self and short-term invisibility add capabilities which the Druid can’t provide on their own, and Hidden Step likely works while using Wild Shape."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Any amount of innate spellcasting is nice, but the Firbolg’s spell options do\n\tlittle to complement the capabilities of a typical fighter. Perhaps the best\n\tcombination is a Dexterity-based fighter with similar skills to a rogue (the\n\tCriminal background should suffice), and the innate spellcasting can make you\n\tfeel a bit like an arcane trickster. Of course, at that point just play an\n\tarcane trickster."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Monks can’t replicate the effects of Detect Magic or Disguise Self, and the\n\tMonk’s dependence on Wisdom works well with Wisdom-based spellcasting (though\n\tyour save DC doesn’t matter much for the Firbolg’s spells). Hidden Step is\n\tespecially appealing for a class which is frequently good at stealth but can’t\n\tturn invisible, though it does compete for the Monk’s already over-burdened\n\tBonus Action."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Similar to the Fighter, the Firbolg’s innate spellcasting offers some\n\tcapabilities normally not available to the Paladin, though not frequently\n\tuseful. Speech of Beast and Leaf may be effective due to the Paladin’s need\n\tfor Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Access to Disguise Self and invisiiblity via Hidden Step are tempting for a\n\tclass which often replaces a rogue. Speech of Beast and Leaf may not be useful\n\tsince most rangers need to dump Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Likely the Firbolg’s best martial option, the Firbolg’s innate spellcasting\n\toffers capabilities which generally only the Arcane Trickster can provide.\n\tEven arcane tricksters benefit, getting access to spells which they might not\n\twant to learn with their limited number of spells known. Speech of Beast and\n\tLeaf is helpful, and even if you dumped Charisma you can put Expertise into\n\tPersuasion and you should do fine."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Sorcerer desperately needs to know more spells, so innate spellcasting is\n\talways welcome. Speech of Beast and Leaf is useful, too, thanks to the\n\tSorcerer’s dependence on Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Innate spellcasting is always welcome on the Warlock due to their extremely limited number of spell slots. Speech of Beast and Leaf is useful, too, thanks to the Warlock’s dependence on Charisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Wizard can already replicate all of the Firbolg’s innate spellcasting,\n\tand Speech of Beast and Leaf isn’t appealing on a class that typically dumps\n\tCharisma."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Firbolg Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-classic",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-classic-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Strength increase is just barely enough, but nothing else about the Firbolg\n\tsupports the Barbarian."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Bard needs a Dexterity increase, a Charisma increase, or both. The\n\tFirbolg gets neither."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Despite their flavor text, the Firbolg makes a better cleric than a druid.\n\tDruids can rely on Wisdom for melee attacks thanks to Shillelagh, but Clerics\n\tare stuck using Strength or Dexterity. A Strength increase makes that much\n\teasier, allowing for a wide range of melee cleric builds. If you still want a\n\tdruidic feel, Nature is a great option."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Wisdom is the only thing that the Druid strictly needs, so the Firbolg is a\n\tnatural choice both mechanically and thematically. A Strength increase might\n\ttempt you into melee, but if you’re going that route Shillelagh and Wild Shape\n\tare better options than trying to rely on Strength."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Strength increase is just barely enough, but nothing else about the Firbolg\n\tsupports the Fighter."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Wisdom is great, but the Monk’s primary role is as a Striker so they can’t\n\tafford to lag defensively by not getting a Dexterity increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Strength increase is just barely enough, but nothing else about the Firbolg\n\tsupports the Paladin."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity is typically the Ranger’s primary ability score, but in medium\n\tarmor the Ranger can work with Strength. The Firbolg’s innate spellcasting\n\toffers some useful magical and stealth options which the Ranger normally can’t\n\tprovide, offering new stealth options to help compensate for your relatively\n\tpoor Dexterity."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Firbolg’s innate spellcasting is neat for a rogue, but it’s simply not\n\tenough to make the Rogue a good option since you don’t get a Dexterity\n\tincrease."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
